<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Protectorate of Southern Nigeria</h1><p class="st-meta">1900 CE – 1914 CE · capital Calabar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British protectorate over south-eastern Nigeria, later joined with Lagos, that formed the southern half of Nigeria before amalgamation in 1914.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 1 January 1900 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> combined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/niger-coast-protectorate">Niger Coast Protectorate</a> with the southern lands of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/royal-niger-company-territories">Royal Niger Company</a> to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> of Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, under the high commissioner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralph-moor">Ralph Moor</a>, with headquarters at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/old-calabar">Old Calabar</a>. It took over the delta trading states, the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibibio-people">Ibibio</a>, and the remains of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The new administration conquered the interior by force, most notably in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-aro-war">Anglo-Aro War</a> of 1901-1902, which destroyed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aro-confederacy">Aro Confederacy</a> and its oracle. It built roads and courts, recruited labour and promoted the export of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> and palm kernels. Christian missions expanded rapidly, spreading schools among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> and other peoples. In 1906 it merged with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lagos-colony">Lagos Colony</a>, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lagos">Lagos</a> became its capital under Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-egerton">Walter Egerton</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>On 1 January 1914 the Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> was amalgamated with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-northern-nigeria">Protectorate of Northern Nigeria</a> to form <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colonial-nigeria">Colonial Nigeria</a>, while remaining a separate group of provinces. The southern provinces later became the Western and Eastern Regions of the Nigerian federation.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/protectorate-of-southern-nigeria.jpg" alt="Protectorate of Southern Nigeria" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Southern_Nigeria_Protectorate_(1900%E2%80%931914).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1900 CE – 1914 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Calabar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ralph Moor, Walter Egerton</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, traditional religion, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Igbo, Yoruba, Efik</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Southern Nigeria, Southern Nigeria Protectorate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1907&amp;focus=protectorate-of-southern-nigeria" aria-label="Protectorate of Southern Nigeria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4774 1936 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Protectorate of Southern Nigeria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5094 2192L5094 2192M5094 2192L5195 2204M5094 2192L5219 2232M5094 2192L5175 2243M5094 2192L4997 651M5094 2192L5094 2192"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5094 2192h0M5195 2204h0M5219 2232h0M5175 2243h0M4997 651h0M5094 2192h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5094 2192h0"/><path